WEBVTT

00:00:14.106 --> 00:00:17.989
ConvexLLM listens to speech, and answers in text.

00:00:19.389 --> 00:00:24.786
Most speech language models feed audio
into a text LLM through a learned adapter.

00:00:25.086 --> 00:00:30.590
Its outputs can land where no text token lives,
and the decoder has to learn to read them.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.261
ConvexLLM replaces the adapter with C-Gate.

00:00:35.561 --> 00:00:42.345
For each audio position, it picks 16 rows of the
LLM's frozen embedding table, and weights them.

00:00:42.645 --> 00:00:45.376
The weights are non-negative and sum to one,

00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:49.536
so the result always stays inside
the hull of real text embeddings.

00:00:50.936 --> 00:00:56.589
Position by position, speech becomes a path
through embeddings the LLM already knows.

00:01:02.258 --> 00:01:09.000
The full model pairs a Whisper-large-v3
encoder with a Qwen3.5-9B decoder.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.121
Text embeddings and the output head stay
frozen in all three training stages.

00:01:15.821 --> 00:01:24.930
On VoiceBench, ConvexLLM averages 75.36,
ahead of Qwen2.5-Omni and MiMo-Audio.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.388
The largest gains come on
spoken knowledge and reasoning.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:33.402
And on MMAU test-mini, it reaches 62.5%.

00:01:34.902 --> 00:01:40.812
ConvexLLM. Code and model weights are
coming soon, and the paper is on arXiv.
